  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Gulzar Ahmad Bhat vs Union Territory Of J&K on 30 May, 2024 Author: Sindhu Sharma Bench: Sindhu Sharma High Court Of Jammu & Kashmir And Ladakh At Srinagar Reserved on: 1 Pronounced on: 3 WP(Crl) No. CrlM No. Gulzar Ahmad Bhat S/o Gh. Nabi Bhat, R/o Nihama, Tehsil Kakapora, District Pulwama,6 through his wife-Rafiqa Akhter (38 years) W/o Gulzar Ahmad Bhat, R/o Nihama, Tehsil Kakapora, District Pulwama .... Petitioner/A Through:- Mr. Tuba Manzoor, Advocate. V/s 1. Union Territory of J&K, Through Principal Secretary, Home Department, J&K Govt., Civil Secretariat, Srinagar/Jammu 2. District Magistrate, Pulwama 3. Senior Superintendent of Police, Pulwama 4. Superintendent, Central Jail, Kotbhalwal, Jammu .....Res Through:- Mr. Zahid Qais Noor, G.A. Coram: Hon'Ble Mrs. Justice Sindhu Sharma, Judge Judgment Gulzar Ahmad Bhat vs Union Territory Of J&K on 30 May, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/115370757/ 1 02.",
    "facts": "01. This petition has been preferred against the detention order No.30/ DMP/PSA/23 dated 12.05.2023, passed by the District Magistrate, Pulwama, whereby, the detentue, namely, Gulzar Ahmad Bhat, S/o Gh. Nabi Bhat, R/o Nihama Tehsil Kakapora District Pulwama, has been detained under Section 8 of the Jammu and Kashmir Public Safety Act, 1978, with a view to prevent him from acting in any manner prejudicial to the security of the State. This order of detention has been assailed by the detenue through his wife, Rafiqa Akhter. The detenue has challenged the order of detention on the grounds that: - i. The allegations in the grounds of detention are vague and mere assertions of the Detaining Authority against which no prudent man can make an effective representation. ii. There is not even a single activity based on the material which could constitute the grounds of detention against the detenue. iii. The detenue was taken into custody by Police Station Kakapora and was bound down under Section 107 and 151 of CrPC for maintaining good behavior, and this condition has not been violated by the detenue. iv. The Detaining Authority without assigning any compelling reason has passed the order of detention. v. The material relied upon while framing the grounds of detention has not been provided to the detenue by the Detaining Authority which has prevented the detenue from making an effective representation. vi. The respondents have not observed the procedural safeguards as provided under Jammu and Kashmir Public Safety Act as well as Constitution of India. vii. The Advisory Board has not heard the detenue before conforming the order of detention.",
    "arguments": ""
  },
